{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"e61266e631d248257c3502d853548b84981207e760271434d7446848ad9efb9b","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"initial_bytes_b64":"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA==","role":"output"}],"case_id":"a030fd374596bdfd09b347fee124b6ee033683ca084d3a5fabac74b35ce6202b","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[8,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[8,1,1]},"entry":"kernel_large_vector_dynamic_lane","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"a-dynamic-lane-of-an-eight-lane-vector","output":{"binding":0,"kind":"buffer","length":64,"offset":0},"rationale":"A dynamic lane index into an EIGHT-lane vector. Vulkan's `OpVectorExtractDynamic` / `OpVectorInsertDynamic` only cover two-, three- and four-component vectors, so a wider one is emitted as an array and the dynamic lane is emulated: `emitter::body::core_inst::emit_large_vector_dynamic_extract` builds a chain of `OpIEqual` / `OpSelect` over every lane, and `emit_large_vector_dynamic_insert` does the same over eight `OpCompositeInsert` candidates. 93 corpus sources contain a dynamic `extractelement` on an `<8 x ...>` and not one authored case reached either function; measured with per-function coverage against every cased source, this fixture reaches both plus `dynamic_index_const`, and no cased source reaches any of the three.\n\nThe emulation is a select chain, so what has to be checked is that each lane's comparison selects the lane it names and that the fall-through default is lane 0. Every thread uses a different insert position (`tid & 7`) and a different extract position (`(tid * 5) & 7`, a full permutation because 5 is coprime with 8), so across the dispatch each of the eight arms of BOTH chains is the selected one exactly once. Four threads extract the lane they just inserted and four extract an untouched one, so a chain that selected the wrong operand of `OpSelect`, or that compared against the wrong lane constant, moves a specific word rather than all of them.\n\nThe `.ll` is hand-written and the `.metal` is a different spelling of the same computation: the module builds `<8 x i32>` in SSA and uses `insertelement`/`extractelement`, while the Metal source writes and reads a `thread uint v[8]`. Both start from the same eight constants (1, 11, 21, 31, 41, 51, 61, 71), write `tid + 1000` at the same position and read the same two positions; only the storage spelling differs. Simulating each independently gives (1000, 1000), (51, 1001), (1002, 1002), (71, 1003), (1004, 1004), (11, 1005), (1006, 1006), (31, 1007) -- so Metal matching is a check that the emulated chain agrees with a derivation it never ran. Every value is a small exact integer and the buffer starts all-zero, which no arm can produce.\n\n`loop_free`: the eight-element initializer is written out rather than looped, so the module has no branch at all. One threadgroup of eight threads, one per lane.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"e61e7da25124cf1f17af8cedac57ff0dd188b8eb5c2d2084efa63860ea1d361d","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"bytes_b64":"","role":"input"},{"binding":1,"bytes_b64":"AAAAPAA4AAAAOAA4ADgAAAA8AAAANAAAADAANgA5AAAALAAoAC4AAFU1VDlmLgAA/zsAFGYyAACaOc00MzcAAGYuMzvNOAAAZiqaO5o1AAAAJOA7ADgAAGY2zThmMgAAZjpmMpo5AAAANAA0ADoAAB8l1zsKNwAACi8fO0g1AAA=","role":"input"},{"binding":2,"initial_bytes_b64":"q6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6ur","role":"output"}],"case_id":"9311fd5e8c5539ec223f89a825041f080157b51d1340d0149eeb31909217fc2d","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[16,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[16,1,1]},"entry":"kernel_pack_unorm565_ties","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"the-rgb565-pack-at-the-exact-tie","output":{"binding":2,"kind":"buffer","length":132,"offset":0},"rationale":"`pack_float_to_unorm565` and `pack_half_to_unorm565` over sixteen lanes of buffer-supplied operands the Metal compiler cannot fold. Six of the words land on an EXACT tie: each component's f32 product with its field maximum is precisely k + 0.5 with k even, so rounding to nearest EVEN answers k and rounding ties away from zero answers k + 1, and every one of the three fields diverges at once. SPIR-V's Round leaves the tie direction to the implementation while RoundEven does not, and SPIRV-Cross spells the two as different MSL functions -- `round` and `rint` -- so the choice is observable. Ties on ODD k sit beside them as controls, since both rules answer k + 1 there; k = 30 and k = 62 put a tie at the top of the 5-bit and 6-bit fields, where rounding away would also be the only way to reach the field maximum. The half source cannot reach a discriminating tie at all -- the only half whose product with 31 or 63 is exactly k + 0.5 is 0.5 itself, and 15.5 and 31.5 round up under both rules -- so its sixteen lanes carry ordinary values and pin that the arithmetic happens at float width after the widen. The remaining float lanes hold the ends of the normalized range and values outside it that the clamp must bring back. The 33rd word is left at the 0xABABABAB fill and read back as an overrun guard.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"e69731a0424e5b6c64c013bd65c0d76fbe9ec6f69423900b4676bff634d96371","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"bytes_b64":"CgAAAAwAAAALAAAAFAAAABUAAAAXAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA","role":"input"},{"binding":1,"initial_bytes_b64":"q6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6urq6s=","role":"in_out"}],"case_id":"c1f001472c480bc1185150415a613f9090e5bb91bb217c33875d3c0a7ca0d5ba","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[1,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[1,1,1]},"entry":"TonemappingRange_int3","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"collapse-an-int3-range-to-its-outer-min-and-max","output":{"binding":1,"kind":"buffer","length":68,"offset":0},"rationale":"min=(10,12,11) and max=(20,21,23) give per-channel extents (10,9,12); min3 of those is 9 and max3 is 12, so 9*10 > 12 selects the rebalancing branch. There max3(min)=12 is not greater than min3(max)=20, so the shader collapses both bounds to scalars: min3(min)=10 and max3(max)=23, broadcast across xyz with w zero. Every one of the six min3/max3 calls is evaluated, and both the tonemapping and histogram sub-structs receive the same pair, so the poisoned 68-byte output is observed byte-exactly.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_references":[],"visible_function_tables":[]}
{"acceleration_structures":[],"air_sha256":"e6efc133bb252e7c90ebd759ce91ba15096f27d91e4a155152ff22c3d0a1b341","argument_buffer_buffers":[],"argument_buffer_intersection_function_tables":[],"argument_buffer_textures":[],"authored_by":"claude:opus-5","buffers":[{"binding":0,"initial_bytes_b64":"","role":"in_out"},{"binding":1,"bytes_b64":"AAAAAAAAAIAAAAA/AACAPwAAwD8AAABAAAAgQAAAQEAAAGBAAAAAvwAAgL8AAMC/AAAgwAAAQMAAAIDAAACAPgAAQD8AAIC+AACgP5qZmT4zMzM/d8wrMgAAKEEAAMlCgJYYS4CWGMsAAABLAACAS+x4rWAAAIB/AACA/wAAwH8=","role":"input"}],"case_id":"51682dbee8e59dce2e189d494962b04a4a0d7f523e1260764c342ee97b788bef","compare":{"kind":"exact"},"device_buffer_arrays":[],"dispatch":{"grid":[1,1,1],"threads_per_threadgroup":[1,1,1]},"entry":"kernel_pi_scaled_sin_cos","execution_safety":"loop_free","function_constants":[],"intersection_function_tables":[],"kernel_stage_inputs":[],"name":"pi-scaled-sine-and-cosine-are-exact-at-every-half-integer","output":{"binding":0,"kind":"buffer","length":260,"offset":0},"rationale":"sinpi and cospi are not sin(float(pi)*x) and cos(float(pi)*x), and this case is the device evidence for the argument reduction that replaced that pre-multiply. float(pi) is 3.1415927410125732, which sits 8.74e-08 above pi, so the scaled argument overshoots every zero crossing: the old lowering answered sinpi(1) as -8.74e-08 where Metal answers -0.0, cospi(100.5) as -1.03e-05 where Metal answers 0.0. Worse, the scaled argument is LARGE, and MSL's sin and cos flush to 0.0 above float(pi/2)*2^22, so cospi(1e7) came back 0.0 where Metal answers 1.0 -- a full-scale error, not a rounding one. Of the 64 lanes below the old lowering got 29 wrong.\\n\\nBoth functions have period 2 and 2 is a power of two, so the reduction is exact and the anchors are exact with it. Sixteen of the 32 arguments are multiples of 0.5, where the reduced residue is exactly 0 and the answer must be an exact 0 or +-1; those lanes are the whole point. The two signed-zero conventions are measured, not assumed, and they are NOT the same for the two functions: sinpi returns -0.0 at every odd integer (1, 3, -1, -3) and +0.0 at every even one, while cospi returns +0.0 at every half-integer without exception, including 0.5, 2.5, -1.5, 10.5 and 100.5, whose quadrant arm is a negated sine and would otherwise be -0.0.\\n\\nThe large arguments are the second half of the case. 1e7, -1e7, 2^23, 2^24 and 1e20 are all past the point where every float is an even integer, so sinpi is 0 and cospi is 1 there, and the old lowering answered 0.0 for all five cosines. 10.5 and 100.5 are large enough for the scaled form to drift visibly but small enough to still carry a fraction. 1e-08 is the other end: sinpi of it is 3.1415926e-08, which is pi times the argument, so the small-argument slope is pinned too.\\n\\nThe three non-finite arguments answer the canonical quiet NaN 0x7fc00000 for both functions, measured for +inf, -inf and NaN alike. The old lowering answered 0.0 for all six, because the reduction's x - x is a NaN and MSL's sin flushes a NaN to zero; the reduction now guards on |x| < inf and selects the NaN constant.\\n\\n0.3, 0.7, 0.25, 0.75, -0.25 and 1.25 are the ordinary regime, where the answer is an irrational number and the case is asserting agreement rather than an identity. Points where Metal and this translator differ in the last bit were deliberately EXCLUDED: at 0.1, 1.7, -2.3, 0.9999999, 1.0000001 and 2.9999998 the two disagree by exactly one ULP, and neither is the wrong one -- checked against float64, Metal is correctly rounded at three of those and this translator is correctly rounded at the other four. That last ULP is the floor for any implementation that is not bit-copying Metal's polynomial, and it is not what this case is about.\\n\\nThe 65th word is left at the 0xABABABAB fill and read back as an overrun guard.","render_targets":[],"samplers":[],"stage":"kernel","texture_arrays":[],"textures":[],"threadgroup_memory":[],"vertex_inputs":[],"visible_function_refer